mcprack (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Harden mcprack for public-internet deployment: CSRF protection
    (Flask-WTF) on every state-changing form, rate-limited /login
    (Flask-Limiter), secure/httponly/samesite session cookies, security
    headers (CSP, X-Frame-Options, HSTS) via a new after_request hook,
    and a startup guard refusing to boot with the insecure default
    SECRET_KEY.
  * Add STRICT_SERVER_PERMISSIONS flag for fail-closed per-user MCP
    server access (default off, preserves existing installs).
  * Gate the /login query-param username/password prefill behind
    DEMO_MODE, so it can't leak a real password into browser history or
    access logs outside the public demo.
  * Fix ProxyFix to also trust X-Forwarded-For (x_for=1), so audit log
    entries record the real client IP instead of the reverse proxy's.
  * Add systemd sandboxing directives to mcprack.service
    (ProtectSystem=strict, resource limits, etc.), transitively
    containing spawned per-user MCP proxy subprocesses.
  * Add a sample Apache reverse-proxy vhost (debian/apache-mcprack.conf)
    covering TLS, HSTS, and forwarded headers, shipped as package
    documentation.
  * Document known internet-deployment limitations (proxy token
    revocation, admin-compromise blast radius) and note optional
    OAuth/OIDC login as a future improvement, in README and
    debian/README.Debian.

 -- CyberVitexus <info@vitexsoftware.cz>  Thu, 30 Jul 2026 18:16:23 +0200

mcprack (0.2.19) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Raise HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT default 12s -> 15s (and LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT
    20s -> 22s to match): live-verified on mcprack.vitexsoftware.com
    that 12s was not a safe margin for every backend -- a cold spawn of
    a server that does its own outbound auth handshake as part of
    startup (multiflexi-mcp-server, against a remote API) succeeded at
    11.2s in one run and hit the 12s ceiling and failed in another. 15s
    leaves real headroom while staying comfortably under gunicorn's 30s
    worker timeout.

 -- CyberVitexus <info@vitexsoftware.cz>  Tue, 28 Jul 2026 19:30:29 +0200

mcprack (0.2.18) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Raise the per-user proxy handshake timeout from a hardcoded 5s to a
    (now env-overridable) 12s default: a cold 'fastmcp run ... --
    transport http' reliably takes ~6s to bind its port and answer a
    health probe, which the old 5s budget never allowed for, so every
    cold spawn on a host this slow failed with a false 'failed its
    startup handshake' error even though the backend was fine. Also
    raise LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT from 15s to 20s to keep it comfortably above
    the new handshake budget. Both are now overridable via
    MCP_PROXY_HANDSHAKE_TIMEOUT / MCP_PROXY_LOCK_WAIT_TIMEOUT in
    /etc/mcprack/env.

 -- CyberVitexus <info@vitexsoftware.cz>  Tue, 28 Jul 2026 18:39:47 +0200

mcprack (0.2.18) unstable; urgency=medium

  * wsgi.py: wrap the app in werkzeug's ProxyFix (x_proto=1, x_host=1) so
    it trusts X-Forwarded-Proto/-Host from the Apache reverse proxy.
    Without it, url_for(..., _external=True) - used for the proxy/mcp
    URLs handed to MCP clients - generated http:// URLs even when served
    over https, since the Apache<->gunicorn hop itself is plain HTTP.

